exercises.solutionCode:
const http = require('http');
const fs = require('fs/promises');

const server = http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
  if (req.url === '/report') {
    const data = await fs.readFile('./big-report.csv');
    res.end(`Report size: ${data.length} bytes`);
  } else {
    res.end('OK');
  }
});

server.listen(3000);

sections.body:
Long before Promises existed in JavaScript, Node needed some convention for handing back the result of an asynchronous operation, and it settled on a simple rule followed almost universally across the core API and the wider ecosystem for years: **every callback's first parameter is reserved for an error**, and every remaining parameter carries the actual result. By convention you call it (err, data), and the very first thing you do inside the callback is check whether err is truthy:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

fs.readFile('./config.json', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error('Failed to read config:', err.message);
    return;
  }
  console.log('Config contents:', data);
});
```

It's worth understanding *why* this convention exists rather than treating it as an arbitrary style rule. With a normal synchronous function, you'd wrap a risky call in try { ... } catch (err) { ... }, and that works because the call stack containing your try block is still alive when the error would be thrown. An asynchronous callback doesn't have that luxury: by the time fs.readFile's work actually finishes and it's ready to report a result, the original call to fs.readFile(...) has long since returned, and the stack frame containing any try/catch you might have wrapped around it is completely gone. A throw inside the callback later on would have nowhere meaningful to be caught by that original code. So Node's core APIs sidestep the problem entirely: instead of throwing, they simply *pass* the error as data, as the first argument to the one function they know is guaranteed to run when the operation completes -- your callback. Skipping the if (err) check is the single most common way real bugs slip through error-first callback code, because doing so doesn't crash anything -- it just silently proceeds to use data, which may be undefined or garbage.

Chain a few dependent asynchronous steps together with callbacks -- read a file, then use its contents to query a database, then use that result to write another file -- and you get nested callbacks inside callbacks inside callbacks, colloquially called 'callback hell' or the 'pyramid of doom':

```javascript
fs.readFile('./user-id.txt', 'utf8', (err, id) => {
  if (err) return handleError(err);
  db.getUser(id.trim(), (err, user) => {
    if (err) return handleError(err);
    db.getOrders(user.id, (err, orders) => {
      if (err) return handleError(err);
      fs.writeFile('./orders.json', JSON.stringify(orders), (err) => {
        if (err) return handleError(err);
        console.log('Done.');
      });
    });
  });
});
```

The indentation creep is the visible symptom, but the actual costs run deeper. First, error handling has to be repeated, correctly, at every single level -- miss one if (err) return ... anywhere in that chain, and an error at that step gets silently swallowed while execution incorrectly falls through to code that assumes success. Second, this shape is genuinely hard to *compose*: there's no simple way to say 'run these three independent lookups concurrently and continue once all three finish' with raw callbacks -- you'd have to hand-roll a counter that tracks how many of N parallel callbacks have fired yet, and handle the case where more than one fails. Third, it's hard to *reuse* -- you can't easily extract 'the middle three steps' into their own named function and pass it around the way you can with an expression that evaluates to a value. Promises, and later async/await, were designed specifically to solve these three problems, not merely to look prettier.

async/await is syntax sugar layered directly on top of Promises -- it does not introduce a new concurrency mechanism, and understanding it as 'Promises with different punctuation' will save you from a lot of confused debugging. Marking a function async guarantees two things: it always returns a Promise (even if you write return 5;, callers get a Promise that resolves to 5, not the number 5 itself), and inside its body, you're allowed to use the await keyword in front of any Promise-returning expression. await somePromise pauses that async function's execution at that exact line until the Promise settles, then either hands back the resolved value (as if it were a plain synchronous expression) or throws the rejection reason -- which means you get to use ordinary try/catch, the same syntax you already know:

```javascript
async function processUser() {
  try {
    const id = await fs.readFile('./user-id.txt', 'utf8');
    const user = await db.getUser(id.trim());
    const orders = await db.getOrders(user.id);
    await fs.writeFile('./orders.json', JSON.stringify(orders));
    console.log('Done.');
  } catch (err) {
    handleError(err);
  }
}
```

This is the exact same four-step pipeline as the Promise-chain version above, just written to read like ordinary sequential code -- which is precisely the point. Two mistakes show up constantly once people start using await, and both are worth internalizing now. First: **forgetting await.** const user = db.getUser(id); (missing await) doesn't wait for anything -- user is immediately bound to a *Promise object*, not the eventual user data, and code that goes on to read user.id will throw or silently produce undefined, right away, because the Promise almost certainly hasn't resolved yet. Worse, if that un-awaited Promise later rejects and nothing is attached to handle it, Node reports an 'unhandled promise rejection' -- and since Node 15, this actually **crashes the process** by default, rather than just logging a warning. Second: **accidentally serializing work that should run in parallel.** Writing await inside a loop, once per item, runs every iteration strictly one after another, waiting for each to fully finish before starting the next -- which is often not what you want if the operations are independent:

```javascript
// Serial: each request waits for the previous one to finish first
for (const id of userIds) {
  const user = await db.getUser(id);   // waits here, every single time
  results.push(user);
}

// Parallel: all requests fire together, we wait for all of them at once
const results = await Promise.all(userIds.map(id => db.getUser(id)));
```

Both versions are 'correct' async/await code with no syntax errors and no missing awaits -- the difference is purely about whether you intended sequential dependency between iterations or not, which is a design decision await-in-a-loop makes for you by default, often unintentionally.

examples.code:
const fs = require('fs');

fs.readFile('./settings.json', 'utf8', (err, contents) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error('Could not read settings:', err.message);
    return;
  }
  const settings = JSON.parse(contents);
  console.log('Loaded settings for:', settings.username);
});

async function getUserOrderCount(userId) {
  const user = db.getUser(userId); // BUG: missing await
  return user.orders.length;       // TypeError: cannot read properties of undefined
}

getUserOrderCount(42).catch(err => console.error('Failed:', err.message));

// Sequential: roughly 3x a single lookup's latency, one request waits for the last
async function getOrdersSequential(userIds) {
  const results = [];
  for (const id of userIds) {
    results.push(await db.getOrders(id));
  }
  return results;
}

// Parallel: roughly 1x a single lookup's latency, all requests fire together
async function getOrdersParallel(userIds) {
  return Promise.all(userIds.map(id => db.getOrders(id)));
}

